---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/business/expert/scaling-and-organisational-design">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an expert-level Business lesson, and it assumes you arrive carrying real baggage from
    the earlier tiers: <em>People and organisations</em> (intermediate) gave you coordination,
    incentives and hiring as first-class business problems; <em>Strategy and competitive
    advantage</em> (advanced) asked why some firms stay profitable for decades; and
    <em>Entrepreneurship and venture funding</em> (advanced) covered the startup end of the
    journey this lesson follows into maturity. Here we study what happens to all of that when
    headcount multiplies — the point where every mechanism that worked at ten people quietly
    stops working at a hundred.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>price the coordination load of an organisation with the n(n−1)/2 curve and explain why structure is forced by arithmetic, not fashion;</li>
    <li>diagnose where a growing firm sits in Greiner's five-phase model and name the structural cure for its current crisis;</li>
    <li>choose a legal form and structure (functional, divisional, matrix) as deliberate bets about what matters next;</li>
    <li>design motivation, hiring and communication systems that survive the loss of founder heroics;</li>
    <li>explain why written-first cultures and documented process — not more meetings — are how quality survives headcount.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Scaling a company is <strong>redesigning it repeatedly without stopping the machinery</strong>.
    The 10-person startup's superpower — everyone talks to everyone — becomes the 200-person
    company's failure mode, because communication paths grow as n(n−1)/2: at 10 people, 45
    relationships; at 100, 4,950. Organisational design is the deliberate replacement of hallway
    conversation with structures that scale: classification, growth strategy, legal form,
    objectives, motivation, management, hiring and communication. The unit's nine topics map
    exactly onto that redesign sequence, and each section below takes one topic at its scaling
    hinge — the point where what worked at the previous size quietly stops working.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="coordination maths, structure, process, culture transmission" widget="equationStep" config={scaleCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels in order — they are the lesson's
    argument in miniature. Panel 1 prices the problem: watch the communication-path count explode
    as n grows, and note that no management fashion changes the arithmetic. Panel 2 is the first
    response (structure follows strategy), panels 3 and 4 the maintenance work — processes and
    rituals — without which the structure decays back into hallway conversation. By the last step
    you should see that scaling is not one decision but a repeating cycle of these four moves.
  </p>

  <h2>Business activity: what the company actually does, at 100× size</h2>
  <p>
    Scaling begins with a definition: what business are we in, and what is our added value?
    Adam Smith's pin factory (1776) supplied the founding arithmetic — ten specialised workers
    produced about 48,000 pins a day, where each working alone would manage a handful — and
    specialisation is still the engine: as headcount grows, work is subdivided and coordination
    becomes the binding constraint. The scaling decision hiding inside "business activity" is
    focus versus sprawl: every new product line multiplies the coordination load the
    n(n−1)/2 curve prices. Companies that grow by accretion discover they are really running
    several firms sharing a logo; the discipline is articulating the core activity precisely
    enough that employees can decide what NOT to do without asking — the function definition
    serves as a decision filter, which is why the bland topic "business activity" is where
    scaling strategy actually starts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the first scaling decision is not "grow" but "define" — a precise
    statement of the core activity that lets employees decide what not to do without asking.
    Every boundary the firm crosses next, sector or geography, charges a management system against
    that definition.
  </p>

  <h2>Classification: crossing the sector and scale lines</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus classification — primary, secondary, tertiary; private, public; local,
    national, multinational — becomes strategy as firms scale, because each boundary crossed
    adds a management system. Growing from local to national adds logistics, pricing by region
    and compliance; going multinational adds currency exposure, tax structures, translation and
    legal entity management — qualitatively new work, not more of the old. Sector lines matter
    too: manufacturers scaling add inventory and safety systems; service firms scaling add
    quality control (their product is people). The classification topic's real lesson is that
    organisational design follows the classification: a firm's structure (functional, divisional
    by product, divisional by geography) is a bet about which classification dimension will
    dominate its next decade — Chandler's structure-follows-strategy principle, applied.
  </p>

  <h2>Enterprise, growth and size: the economics of getting bigger</h2>
  <p>
    Growth arrives two ways: organic (new customers, new products) or external (mergers and
    acquisitions — where a majority of studies find most deals fail to create acquirer value,
    largely because integration is a communication problem wearing a finance costume). The
    promised prize: economies of scale — technical (machines used more fully), purchasing,
    marketing and managerial — which drive unit costs down. The offsetting trap:
    <em>diseconomies</em> of scale — coordination costs, slow decisions, motivation decay —
    which is why bigger is not automatically cheaper. Greiner's growth model (1972) remains the
    classic map: each growth phase ends in a characteristic crisis (leadership, autonomy,
    control, red tape) resolved by a structural revolution. The honest arithmetic of size:
    profitability and craft are choices, not functions of headcount — the "small giants"
    evidence shows firms thriving at deliberate scale ceilings.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Greiner, worked.</strong> The model's five phases map onto real schedules:
    creativity (founders doing everything) ends in a <em>leadership crisis</em> — solved by
    hiring managers; direction through functional hierarchy ends in an <em>autonomy
    crisis</em> (the managers smother the doers) — solved by delegation; decentralised
    divisions end in a <em>control crisis</em> — solved by coordination systems (planning,
    product groups); those end in <em>red tape</em> — the crisis many incumbents never escape,
    resolved (where it is resolved) by collaboration structures, squads, and culture-as-
    control. The practical use is diagnostic: name the crisis you are in, and the structural
    cure follows from the phase — the reason reorganisations cluster at predictable
    headcounts (roughly 30, 100, 300, 1,000 in the folklore) rather than at random.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "scaling is doing more of the same, faster." Each phase of
    scale is qualitatively different: processes that carried 20 people actively break at 200 —
    hiring by intuition, decisions by hallway, quality by founder review. Scaling is repeated
    redesign under load, which is why reorganisations are normal (though each costs months of
    productivity — design deliberately, adjust incrementally).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> growth economics gives you the prize (economies of scale) and the
    trap (diseconomies), and Greiner's model turns both into a schedule you can diagnose against.
    The next sections take that diagnosis into law, objectives, people and structure — the four
    places the cure is actually administered.
  </p>

  <h2>Types of business organisation: changing legal skin as you grow</h2>
  <p>
    Legal form is the scaling wardrobe: sole trader to partnership to private limited company
    (share capital, limited liability, contracts and payroll of a real firm) to public
    limited company (IPO: capital markets at the price of disclosure, quarterly pressure and
    diluted control). Each transition changes governance as much as finance — new fiduciary
    duties, new reporting, new external voices. The alternatives encode different growth
    philosophies: franchising scales a brand with other people's capital (McDonald's model);
    cooperatives scale ownership without external shareholders (Mondragon's federation of
    co-ops, tens of thousands of worker-owners, is the standing existence proof); employee
    ownership (John Lewis in the UK) binds motivation to structure. And the exit choice is
    itself organisational design: Patagonia's 2022 transfer of ownership to a trust and
    nonprofit declared, structurally, that the firm had finished scaling toward capital and was
    optimising for purpose.
  </p>

  <h2>Business objectives and stakeholder objectives: keeping the mission legible</h2>
  <p>
    Objectives are the scaling company's coordinate system: without shared goals, the growing
    headcount optimises locally. The practice ladder: mission (why), strategic objectives
    (where), then cascaded, measurable goals — the OKR system (objectives and key results,
    popularised at Google by John Doerr) being the current standard for making strategy legible
    to thousands of people at once. Stakeholder analysis keeps the goal-set honest: Mendelow's
    power/interest matrix tells you who must be managed closely, kept satisfied, kept informed
    or monitored — and scale changes the map, because a 500-person firm's employees and
    regulators are systematically more powerful than a 10-person firm's. The failure mode is
    drift: objectives multiplied past comprehension, or quietly replaced by whatever is measured
    — which returns every scaling conversation to Goodhart's law.
  </p>

  <h2>Motivating employees at scale</h2>
  <p>
    Motivation systems must scale from personal to structural. In a 10-person firm, the founder
    is the motivator; at 1,000, culture and systems are. Herzberg's distinction (hygiene
    factors prevent dissatisfaction; motivators — achievement, recognition, growth — create
    satisfaction) scales surprisingly well as design guidance: fix pay and conditions at
    market, then invest in the motivators at volume — promotion clarity, meaningful work,
    recognition rituals. Equity spreads ownership motivation (option pools, ESOPs), though
    delta decays with company size and vesting horizons — which is why mature firms lean on
    progression frameworks. The scaling-specific risk is stratification: as layers multiply,
    frontline employees drift from purpose; the countermeasures are ritual (demos, all-hands
    Q&A) and internal mobility. What gets recognised and promoted IS the motivation system's
    announcement — bonuses are the smallest part of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Organisation and management: structure as technology</h2>
  <p>
    Structure is coordination technology, and its settings are span of control, layers,
    centralisation and decomposition. Wide spans (Amazon's "two-pizza teams" — small, autonomous,
    service-boundary-defined units) reduce coordination load by localising decisions;
    narrow spans and many layers add control but slow everything (the classic middle-management
    innovation tax). Dunbar's number (~150 stable human relationships) explains why
    sub-150-person firms can run on familiarity and larger ones cannot: past that line,
    structure must carry what relationships used to. Centralisation versus decentralisation is
    the strategic dial: decentralise where local information matters (sales, product),
    centralise where standards compound (brand, security, data). The hardest transition is the
    first manager-of-managers — the skills genuinely change (delegating through others,
    designing structure) — and skipping it produces the pyramid of titled individual
    contributors every scaling post-mortem describes.
  </p>

  <h2>Recruitment, selection and training: hiring the next thousand</h2>
  <p>
    Hiring is where scale is actually built, and the methods must change with volume. At ten
    people, everyone interviews everyone on instinct; at a thousand, instinct compounds bias
    and noise — so structured interviews (same questions, scoring rubrics, work-sample tests)
    become necessary. Google's internal research famously found structure beats impression for
    predicting performance, and "culture interviews" guard values only if they test
    <em>behaviours</em>, not affinity. Onboarding is training's scaling form: a repeatable
    first-90-days system converts hiring spend into productivity instead of churn. And the
    internal pipeline matters more as external markets tighten: training managers to train
    managers is the compounding investment — a company that promotes on demonstrated delegation
    rather than personal heroics scales its management as fast as its headcount; one that does
    not, stalls at every layer.
  </p>

  <h2>Internal and external communication: documentation beats meetings</h2>
  <p>
    At scale, communication architecture is destiny. The arithmetic (n(n−1)/2) dooms
    all-hands-on-deck; the replacements are written-first cultures: Amazon's six-page narrative
    memos (replacing slide decks, read silently at the start of meetings), Stripe's writing
    culture, decision logs recording owner, date and reasoning — documentation compounds while
    meetings don't. Internal channels need deliberate topology: team-level transparency,
    company-wide amplification, and escalation paths that surface bad news fast (the governance
    lesson applied: punish messengers and you blind the organisation). External communication
    scales differently: brand consistency needs central standards; customer responsiveness
    needs distributed authority — the same centralise/decentralise dial, run on the outside of
    the firm. The measurable dividend of written-first design: onboarding speed, decision
    latency and the ability to run hybrid teams across time zones — communication debt is now
    as quantifiable as technical debt.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "more meetings mean better communication." Meetings are
    synchronous, expensive and non-compounding: they communicate to whoever is present, once.
    Written decisions communicate permanently, asynchronously and at zero marginal cost to the
    next hire — which is why scaling companies convert meeting culture to writing culture as
    deliberately as they convert hallway sales to CRM systems.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "bureaucracy is a dirty word." Weber's rational-legal
  bureaucracy — impersonal rules, documented procedures, merit-based office — was the
  technology that made organisations trustworthy at scale: it is why a thousand-employee
  company can treat customer number 4,001 exactly like customer number 4,002. The failure
  mode is ossification, not bureaucracy itself; the scaling question is keeping the rules
  current, not wishing them away.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>At 45 employees, how many potential communication paths exist, and what structural claim does that arithmetic support?</li>
    <li>A 300-person firm has decentralised into divisions, and executives now complain they have lost control of operations while managers drown in planning procedures. Which Greiner phase and crisis is this, and what was its designated cure?</li>
    <li>Why does structure-follows-strategy (Chandler) make a reorganisation a <em>bet</em> rather than an administrative tidy-up?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) 45 × 44 / 2 = 990 paths — nearly a thousand relationships where
    45 people can each maintain perhaps 150. That gap, not headcount itself, is the case for
    deliberate structure: something must carry the coordination relationships used to carry.
    (2) Greiner's control crisis, closing the delegation phase — the designated cure is
    coordination systems (formal planning, product groups, centralised support functions), which
    is also how the next crisis, red tape, is seeded. (3) Because choosing functional versus
    divisional versus matrix structure is choosing which classification dimension — product,
    geography, function — will dominate the next decade; each option optimises a different
    accountability at the cost of the others. That is a strategy judgement about the future, not
    a tidy-up of the present.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Communication paths grow as n(n−1)/2, so structure is forced by arithmetic — the hallway stops working long before the org chart admits it.</li>
    <li>Structure follows strategy: functional, divisional and matrix forms are bets about which dimension dominates the next decade.</li>
    <li>Greiner's phases end in predictable crises (leadership, autonomy, control, red tape), each with a structural cure — diagnose before reorganising.</li>
    <li>Legal form is scaling wardrobe: each transition (sole trader to PLC) changes governance, not just finance.</li>
    <li>Motivation scales from personal to structural — Herzberg's motivators, rituals and promotion patterns outweigh bonuses.</li>
    <li>Written-first cultures compound; meetings communicate once, to whoever is present.</li>
    <li>Process and rituals are how quality and culture survive headcount — bureaucracy's failure mode is ossification, not existence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Scaling raises the question this lesson deliberately left open: once ownership and control
    separate, who watches the watchers? The sibling expert lesson <em>Corporate governance</em>
    takes up boards, fiduciary duty and the agency problem — the accountability machinery every
    scaled firm eventually grows.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The design frontier.</strong> Organisation design is converging on mechanism
    design: decision rights as explicit contracts (RAPID, DACI), reorganisation as portfolio
    rebalancing, and hybrid/remote work as a topology problem solved with writing and rituals
    rather than presence. The 2020s debate — "founder mode" versus professional management
    (Paul Graham's 2024 essay lit the argument) — is really about which decisions can be
    delegated and which cannot; the honest answer is per-mechanism, not per-ideology.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The failure catalogue.</strong> Scaling post-mortems repeat four failures:
    under-investing in managers-of-managers; process copied from the wrong stage (a 1,000-person
    process on a 50-person company); culture left to osmosis (who gets promoted announces the
    values — Gumroad's founder Sahil Lavingia's 2019 reflection on his own scaling failure is
    the genre's honest centrepiece); and confusing growth with progress (scale ceilings are a
    legitimate choice — Burlingham's "small giants" documents firms that declined to scale and
    outperformed on every metric except headcount).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can AI-flattened organisations (fewer coordination layers,
    more capable individual contributors) raise the effective Dunbar limit? Do remote-first
    companies converge on a stable org design or keep experimenting? And is there a general law
    linking headcount to coordination overhead that founders can price in advance — or is every
    scaling curve learned the expensive way? The research base is growing; the anecdotes are
    still cheaper.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chandler, Strategy and Structure (1962); Meaghan Johnson/RAPID framework (Bain); Burlingham, Small Giants; Sahil Lavingia's "reflecting on my failure" (Gumroad, 2019). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
